          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "**Please get this checked soon, and call emergency services now if it changes.** The automated check labelled this routine because none of its listed patterns matched. That is not a clearance. The red-flag guidance (*Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*, emergency medicine and cardiology society summary) says squeezing or heaviness in the centre of the chest that lasts more than a few minutes, or goes away and comes back, is treated as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise. It also says chest discomfort brought on by less effort than usual needs urgent attention. The hypertension activity guidance (*Physical activity for high blood pressure*) says anyone with chest pain on exertion should be assessed before starting vigorous activity.\n\n**Call emergency services immediately** if you get this pressure at rest, if it lasts more than a few minutes, if it spreads to your arm, jaw, neck or back, or if it comes with breathlessness, cold sweating, nausea, light-headedness or a sense of doom.\n\n**About prevention:** the passages I retrieved do not describe prevention for angina. I cannot tell you what guidance recommends after a diagnosis, so please ask a clinician. The heart failure summary (*Heart failure*) does name coronary artery disease as the most common cause of heart failure, but it does not cover angina treatment or prevention. The reflux summary (*Gastroesophageal reflux disease*) notes that reflux can cause chest pain, but that this kind of pain is less specific and often has another cause, so it should not be assumed without assessment.\n\nOnly a clinician can tell what is causing your symptoms. Please book an appointment soon and describe the pattern you described to me.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
